WEBVTT

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:15.210
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass.
<v Ira Glass>来自芝加哥 WBEZ 电台，这里是《这个美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:19.110
<v Bryan>OK, so listen. Right now, we're going to be a little bit quiet, because--
<v Bryan>好了，听着。现在我们要保持安静，因为——

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:19.950
<v Bryan>[CHILD WHINING]
<v Bryan>[小孩哭闹]

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:22.980
<v Bryan>--we're going to have a family talk. Are you ready for a family talk?
<v Bryan>——我们要开一个家庭会议。你们准备好开家庭会议了吗？

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:24.300
<v Child>Wait, wait, wait, wait, Dad.
<v Child>等等，等等，等等，爸爸。

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:24.960
<v Bryan>What?
<v Bryan>什么事？

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:35.190
<v Ira Glass>Bryan LeBaron is dad of a big family, seven kids in all. He mostly grew up in the United States, but his father and most of his family is Mexican. And he moved back there a few years ago.
<v Ira Glass>布莱恩·勒巴龙是一个大家庭的父亲，一共有七个孩子。他大部分时间在美国长大，但他父亲和大部分家人都是墨西哥人。几年前他搬回了那里。

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:42.900
<v Ira Glass>Back in November, he gathered everybody together for a family meeting outside on a patio. Most of the kids were in a long bench, holding pillows.
<v Ira Glass>去年十一月，他把大家召集到露台外面开家庭会议。大部分孩子都坐在长凳上，手里拿着枕头。

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:48.900
<v Bryan>Lexie thinks that this is an important moment in our family, and I agree with her.
<v Bryan>莱克西认为这是我们家一个重要的时刻，我同意她的看法。

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:58.830
<v Ira Glass>Lexie is Lexie Harrison-Cripps, the journalist that recorded this meeting. Because she knew what some of the kids did not know-- that Bryan was planning to run for a seat in Mexico's House of Representatives.
<v Ira Glass>莱克西是记录这次会议的记者莱克西·哈里森-克里普斯。因为她知道一些孩子们不知道的事——布莱恩正计划竞选墨西哥众议院议员。

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:00.030
<v Child>What does that mean?
<v Child>那是什么意思？

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:12.580
<v Bryan>I want to work for the government. That's what that means. And since I'm going to be running, it's going to be a lot of work for me, and I'm going to be traveling a lot. I'm going to be away from home a lot.
<v Bryan>我想为政府工作。那就是这个意思。既然我要去参选，我会有很多工作要做，而且我会经常出差。我会经常离家。

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:19.000
<v Bryan>And when we go places, people are going to want pictures. So maybe you're walking with me, and somebody will take a picture of us. We don't know how--
<v Bryan>当我们去一些地方时，人们会想要拍照。所以也许你跟我走在一起，有人会给我们拍照。我们不知道会怎样——

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:20.320
<v Fernando>I'm gonna be famous!
<v Fernando>我要出名了！

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:21.610
<v Fernando>[LAUGHTER]
<v Fernando>[笑声]

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:50.830
<v Bryan>We don't know exactly what that's going to be like, right? But one of the things also is that because of certain things that happened in our family, now there's people in our town that may not want to vote for me. And it might even cause people to want to be mean to our family. OK? And so I wanted to have a talk-- Mom and I wanted to have a talk with you so that you can be ready.
<v Bryan>我们不知道那具体会是什么样，对吧？但其中一件事也是，因为我们家里发生的一些事情，现在我们镇上有些人可能不想投票给我。这甚至可能导致人们想对我们家不友善。好吗？所以我想要谈谈——妈妈和我想要跟你们谈谈，好让你们做好准备。

00:01:50.830 --> 00:02:14.140
<v Ira Glass>Not just ready for the campaign, which is months away, but he's called his family meeting right now, in November, because something's come up that's not only going to affect his candidacy but everyone in the family in the next few days. What's happened is that Macario, their 17-year-old, has just made a big, public declaration on Instagram, telling the world that he's gay, which in some places would be, like, whatever.
<v Ira Glass>不仅要为几个月后的竞选做好准备，他现在——在十一月——就召集了家庭会议，是因为发生了一件事，不仅会影响他的候选人资格，还会在接下来的几天里影响家里的每一个人。发生的事情是，他们 17 岁的儿子马卡里奥刚刚在 Instagram 上做了一个重大且公开的声明，向全世界宣布他是同性恋，这在某些地方可能根本不算什么。

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:40.260
<v Ira Glass>But this family is Mormon, living in a tight-knit fundamentalist community in Mexico. Macario and the other kids go to a fundamentalist Mormon school that very much disapproves of homosexuality. Bryan figured kids at school might start saying cruel and hateful stuff to Macario and his brothers and sisters. Already, some classmates were posting pretty awful comments on Instagram. Bryan wanted all of his children to be prepared.
<v Ira Glass>但这个家庭是摩门教徒，生活在墨西哥一个紧密团结的原教旨主义社区里。马卡里奥和其他孩子在一所原教旨主义的摩门教学校上学，该校非常反对同性恋。布莱恩料到学校里的孩子可能会开始对马卡里奥以及他的兄弟姐妹说残酷和充满仇恨的话。已经有一些同学在 Instagram 上发布了相当糟糕的评论。布莱恩希望他所有的孩子都能做好准备。

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:50.250
<v Bryan>OK, guys, so guess what? You know how a lot of times we always went to church and we always hear about people that are going to date and get married?
<v Bryan>好了，孩子们，你们猜怎么着？你们知道很多时候我们总是去教堂，总是听到关于人们要约会和结婚的事情吗？

00:02:50.250 --> 00:03:05.670
<v Ira Glass>Three of the kids listening to this are young. There's nine-year-old Ahriella, seven-year-old Chanel, and four-year-old Franco. And what that means is that before Bryan can tell them "your brother is gay," he first has to explain "gay," the whole idea of gayness, to these little kids.
<v Ira Glass>听这话的三个孩子还很小。有九岁的阿里埃拉、七岁的香奈儿和四岁的弗兰科。这意味着在布莱恩能对他们说“你哥哥是同性恋”之前，他必须先向这些小家伙们解释什么是“同性恋”，以及同性恋的整个概念。

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:13.170
<v Bryan>Chanel, this is more for you and Ahriella, OK? Have you guys ever heard of the term "gay"?
<v Bryan>香奈儿，这主要是说给你和阿里埃拉听的，好吗？你们听过“同性恋”这个词吗？

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:13.740
<v Child 1>A what?
<v Child 1>什么？

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:14.730
<v Bryan>Gay.
<v Bryan>同性恋。

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:15.450
<v Child 1>Oh, yeah.
<v Child 1>哦，听过。

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:17.440
<v Bryan>What does it mean?
<v Bryan>它是什么意思？

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:17.940
<v Child 2>Gay means--
<v Child 2>同性恋的意思是——

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:18.660
<v Bryan>Yeah.
<v Bryan>对。

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:22.430
<v Child 2>--like, people that, like, wear necklaces that are meant for girls.
<v Child 2>——就像，有人，喜欢戴那种专属于女孩的项链。

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:25.210
<v Bryan>So boys that wear necklaces that are for girls?
<v Bryan>所以是男孩戴女孩戴的项链？

00:03:25.210 --> 00:03:26.320
<v Ira Glass>Good guess, he tells them--
<v Ira Glass>猜得不错，他对他们说——

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:26.820
<v Bryan>That's OK.
<v Bryan>没关系。

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:30.360
<v Ira Glass>--and then launches into his explanation. He keeps it simple.
<v Ira Glass>——然后开始他的解释。他尽量保持简单。

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:36.070
<v Bryan>But when people say the word "gay," usually it means either two boys that like each other--
<v Bryan>但当人们说“同性恋”这个词时，通常它的意思是两个互相喜欢的男孩——

00:03:36.070 --> 00:03:36.610
<v Child 2>I knew it.
<v Child 2>我就知道。

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:55.170
<v Bryan>--and maybe even one day want to get married, or two girls that like each other and that maybe even want to get married. And somebody who feels that way, just like I look at Mom and I think she's beautiful, there can be boys that look at other boys and think they're beautiful. OK? Does that make sense?
<v Bryan>——也许甚至有一天想要结婚，或者两个互相喜欢的女孩，也许甚至想要结婚。而一个有这种感觉的人，就像我看着妈妈，觉得她很漂亮一样，也会有男孩看着其他男孩觉得他们很漂亮。好吗？这讲得通吗？

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:56.080
<v Ira Glass>It's yes all around.
<v Ira Glass>四周传出一片“听懂了”的声音。

00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:56.800
<v Bryan>OK.
<v Bryan>好。

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:58.510
<v Ira Glass>On to the big reveal.
<v Ira Glass>接下来是重大揭晓。

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:12.480
<v Bryan>Well, guess what? Macario-- he thinks that boys are beautiful, and he thinks that he wants to fall in love with a boy. OK? And that's what people call "gay."
<v Bryan>好吧，你们猜怎么着？马卡里奥——他觉得男孩很漂亮，他觉得他想和一个男孩谈恋爱。好吗？这就是人们所说的“同性恋”。

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:13.320
<v Child 1>Mm-hmm.
<v Child 1>嗯哼。

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:21.510
<v Bryan>OK? Now, that doesn't mean that something's wrong with Macario. Macario is perfect just the way that he is, and we love him and we're always going to love him.
<v Bryan>好吗？现在，这并不意味着马卡里奥有什么毛病。马卡里奥本来的样子就很完美，我们爱他，而且我们永远都会爱他。

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:23.550
<v Child 2>But why do they call it "gay"?
<v Child 2>但他们为什么叫它“同性恋”？

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:28.720
<v Bryan>That's just the name they gave it. Why do they call boys "boys" and girls "girls"? There's just a name for everything.
<v Bryan>这就是他们给它起的名字。为什么他们把男孩叫“男孩”，把女孩叫“女孩”？每样东西都有个名字嘛。

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:44.400
<v Ira Glass>I should say that two years ago, this is not how Bryan would have talked about being gay. He was raised to believe that it was a choice and sinful. When Macario came out to him, Macario explained that he had already tried to pray and fast his way out of being gay, and it was impossible.
<v Ira Glass>我得说，两年前，布莱恩绝不会这样谈论同性恋。他从小受到的教育让他相信这是一种选择，是有罪的。当马卡里奥向他出柜时，马卡里奥解释说他已经尝试过通过祈祷和禁食来摆脱同性恋倾向，但这根本不可能。

00:04:44.400 --> 00:05:02.800
<v Ira Glass>And Bryan believed him. He changed how he saw all of it. And as he talks about Macario with the family, Macario is sitting exactly in between his two little sisters as they learn what "gay" means. He smiles, like a mystery contestant on an old-timey game show whose secret identity was just revealed.
<v Ira Glass>而布莱恩相信了他。他改变了对这一切的看法。当他向家人谈起马卡里奥时，马卡里奥正坐在他两个小妹妹中间，听着她们了解“同性恋”的含义。他微笑着，就像老式游戏节目中秘密身份刚刚被揭晓的神秘参赛者。

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:04.720
<v Macario>I didn't really know what was going on.
<v Macario>我其实不知道发生了什么。

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:10.150
<v Ira Glass>And did your dad tell you, OK, here's what we're going to explain to the kids in this meeting?
<v Ira Glass>你爸爸有没有对你说，好吧，这就是我们在这次会议上要向孩子们解释的内容？

00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:18.460
<v Macario>Nope. It was just, I was going to say I was gay, and it literally-- it was not very planned out.
<v Macario>没有。只是，我要说我是同性恋，说实话——这其实没有经过太多计划。

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:32.610
<v Ira Glass>This, of course, is Macario. He says it felt good to hear his dad say all these things in front of everybody. Next, Bryan explained to the little ones in the family the kids in school might be pretty mean about Macario, and we'll need to be ready.
<v Ira Glass>这个人当然就是马卡里奥。他说听到爸爸在所有人面前说出这一切，感觉很好。接着，布莱恩向家里的年幼孩子们解释，学校里的孩子可能会对马卡里奥非常刻薄，我们需要做好准备。

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:37.130
<v Bryan>When people say, oh, your brother is gay, he's going to go to the devil, oh, ha, ha--
<v Bryan>当人们说，哦，你哥哥是同性恋，他要下地狱了，哦，哈，哈——

00:05:37.130 --> 00:05:38.440
<v Child 1>I will say--
<v Child 1>我要说——

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:38.990
<v Bryan>Ha, ha, ha--
<v Bryan>哈，哈，哈——

00:05:38.990 --> 00:05:39.750
<v Child 1>I will say--
<v Child 1>我要说——

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:40.950
<v Bryan>That's what people will say--
<v Bryan>人们会这么说——

00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:42.280
<v Child 1>--that's not true.
<v Child 1>——那不是真的。

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:42.960
<v Lindi>Hey, listen.
<v Lindi>嘿，听着。

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:46.320
<v Bryan>Well, that what's not true? That Macario is gay?
<v Bryan>那什么不是真的？马卡里奥是同性恋吗？

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:48.390
<v Child 1>No, that he's going to go to the devil.
<v Child 1>不是，是说他要下地狱那件事。

00:05:48.390 --> 00:05:53.960
<v Bryan>So I want you to be ready because little kids can be mean sometimes.
<v Bryan>所以我希望你们做好准备，因为小孩子有时会很刻薄。

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:55.320
<v Child 1>Yeah, I know.
<v Child 1>是的，我知道。

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:56.610
<v Child 2>No!
<v Child 2>才不！

00:05:56.610 --> 00:06:01.650
<v Ira Glass>Macario tells them about some of the mean things that kids have said at school. There was this one kid.
<v Ira Glass>马卡里奥向他们讲述了学校里孩子们说过的一些刻薄的话。有这么一个孩子。

00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:25.540
<v Macario>Their dad said they can't do anything to legally kick me out of the town, but they can do everything in their power to make it so that I want to leave on my own. And they just only did that and are doing that because I'm gay. That's the only reason. And I know that they're just doing that because they've been told their whole life that I'm a terrible-- that anyone is a terrible person if they're gay.
<v Macario>他们的爸爸说，他们不能在法律上把我赶出这个镇，但他们可以竭尽全力让我想要主动离开。他们这么做，而且正在这么做，仅仅因为我是同性恋。这就是唯一的理由。我知道他们这么做，只是因为他们一辈子都被灌输这种观念：我是个糟糕透顶的人——如果一个人是同性恋，他就是个糟糕透顶的人。

00:06:25.540 --> 00:06:48.490
<v Macario>So I mean, I get where they're coming from. But like, when they started just saying shit-- I mean, started saying stuff about our friends-- my friends and the people supporting me and then you guys-- like how that guy said that. He was like, no, we can't blame the devil for this. It's their parents' fault. They raised him wrong. They lived in California, Western culture or whatever.
<v Macario>所以我的意思是，我理解他们的出发点。但是当他们开始胡说八道——我是说，开始对我们的朋友——我的朋友、支持我的人以及你们说三道四的时候——就像那个家伙说的那样。他说，不，我们不能为此怪魔鬼。这是他们父母的错。他们把孩子教育坏了。他们住在加利福尼亚，西方的文化什么的。

00:06:48.490 --> 00:07:00.070
<v Macario>When they said that, it just made me so bothered because I'm like, I don't know. You can get mad at me and you can think that I'm this or that, but all these people who are supporting me--
<v Macario>当他们这么说的时候，真的让我很困扰，因为我想，我不知道。你可以生我的气，你可以认为我是这样或那样的人，但所有这些支持我的人——

00:07:00.070 --> 00:07:06.380
<v Ira Glass>So everybody's on the same page, all prepared. Macario is feeling great about it.
<v Ira Glass>所以大家都意见一致，全都做好了准备。马卡里奥对此感觉棒极了。

00:07:06.380 --> 00:07:07.840
<v Child 1>I think it's time for bed.
<v Child 1>我想是时候睡觉了。

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:15.910
<v Bryan>You got that right. OK. I want a hug.
<v Bryan>你说的对。好吧。我要一个抱负。

00:07:15.910 --> 00:07:29.820
<v Ira Glass>And when they go to school after this prep, none of the things that Bryan prepared them for happened. The school made a rule that nobody could talk about Macario. Overall, most of the Mormon community continued to treat Macario like they always had.
<v Ira Glass>在这次心理准备之后，当他们去上学时，布莱恩为他们预作准备的那些事情一件也没有发生。学校制定了一条规则，任何人都不准谈论马卡里奥。总的来说，大部分摩门教社区的人继续像以往一样对待马卡里奥。

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:36.600
<v Ira Glass>All this was a little sad for nine-year-old Ahriella, who marched into school ready to tussle, to defend her beloved brother after the family meeting.
<v Ira Glass>这一切让九岁的阿里埃拉感到有点难过，她为了保卫心爱的哥哥，在家庭会议后斗志昂扬地走进学校准备大干一场。

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:52.290
<v Bryan>She said, well, I walked into class and I just said, hi, everybody, how's it going? Anything new happen yesterday? And they're like, no. And so she said, well, how about the day before? No, nothing new. And she said, hmm, OK.
<v Bryan>她说，好吧，我走进教室，然后就说，嗨，大家好，最近怎么样？昨天有什么新鲜事发生吗？他们说，没有。于是她说，那前天呢？没有，没什么新鲜事。然后她说，嗯，好吧。

00:07:52.290 --> 00:08:07.740
<v Bryan>(LAUGHING) So she just went about her day. So I don't know. It didn't seem to phase the younger kids at school. It was more Macario's classmates. I think the teenagers just maybe shocked by it and not understanding exactly what to think.
<v Bryan>[笑着] 所以她只是继续过她的一天。所以，我不知道。这似乎没有对学校里较小的孩子们产生什么影响。更多的是马卡里奥的同班同学。我认为青少年可能只是对此感到震惊，并不完全明白该怎么想。

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:28.130
<v Ira Glass>In fact, some teenagers set off a pretty frightening array of firecrackers at their house and threw rocks wrapped in pieces of paper with Macario's name written on them, which was scary. Kids and adults posted comments online, like "Macario should find a tall tree and a rope, should die." Bryan and his wife Lindi took all the kids out of the Mormon school.
<v Ira Glass>事实上，一些青少年在他们家外面点燃了一串相当吓人的鞭炮，还扔了用纸包着的石头，纸上写着马卡里奥的名字，这很吓人。孩子和成年人在网上发表评论，比如“马卡里奥应该找一根高树枝和一根绳子，去死吧”。布莱恩和他的妻子林迪把所有的孩子都转出了这所摩门教学校。

00:08:28.130 --> 00:08:43.180
<v Ira Glass>But the family had been prepped. They understood that people might get mad about Macario. And inside the family, the family meeting set the tone. It was a preemptive strike that made Macario's gayness a non-issue, a non-negotiable fact.
<v Ira Glass>但这个家庭已经做好了心理准备。他们明白人们可能会对马卡里奥感到生气。在家庭内部，家庭会议定下了基调。这是一次先发制人的出击，让马卡里奥的同性恋身份不再是个问题，而是一个不容置疑的事实。

00:08:43.180 --> 00:08:55.680
<v Bryan>They really have had little-- not many questions since then. It seemed to really not phase them at all. It was just, OK, you know, that's cool. What are we going to do tomorrow? [LAUGHS]
<v Bryan>从那以后，他们真的很少——没有提太多问题。这似乎对他们一点影响也没有。感觉就是，好吧，你知道，挺酷的。我们明天要做什么？[笑声]

00:08:55.680 --> 00:08:58.050
<v Ira Glass>Can we have a snack? Is there ice cream?
<v Ira Glass>我们能吃零食吗？有冰淇淋吗？

00:08:58.050 --> 00:09:01.200
<v Bryan>That's it, exactly.
<v Bryan>就是这样，完全正确。

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:19.950
<v Ira Glass>Well, today on our program, the power of a family meeting to drop some news-- like your brother's gay-- or the classic one people remember, I guess-- we're getting a divorce. And then there are the administrative family meetings where parents announce some changes in how we do the dishes around here or take out the garbage or whatever, and everybody's going to be doing their fair share.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，今天在我们的节目中，家庭会议具有一种释放重磅消息的力量——比如你哥哥是同性恋——或者大家记忆中的经典例子，我想是——我们要离婚了。然后还有行政性质的家庭会议，父母在会上宣布一些关于我们在家里如何洗碗、倒垃圾或诸如此类的改变，每个人都要尽到自己应尽的份额。

00:09:19.950 --> 00:09:41.240
<v Ira Glass>What a family meeting can and cannot accomplish, especially in families where the kids do not want to go with the parents' plan, that's our show today. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>家庭会议能做到什么，不能做到什么，尤其是在孩子们不想顺从父母计划的家庭里，这就是我们今天的节目。请继续收听。

00:09:41.240 --> 00:10:04.790
<v Ira Glass>Act 1-- A Slow Decision Process That's Mostly But Maybe Not Completely Over. Sometimes when there's a family decision, the family can keep talking about it for months, on and off-- in the car, at meals, whenever-- with everybody weighing in with different points of view. Over the last few months, I've been talking to the parents in one family like that. Back when I first chatted with them in December, I asked the dad in the family how often the topic comes up.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕——一个漫长的决策过程，大部分可能已经结束，但也可能尚未完全结束。有时，当面临家庭决策时，家庭会断断续续地讨论几个月——在车里、在饭桌上、随时随地——每个人都提出不同的观点。在过去的几个月里，我一直在和这样一个家庭的父母交谈。早在十二月我第一次和他们聊天时，我问了这个家庭的爸爸这个话题出现的频率有多高。

00:10:04.790 --> 00:10:17.480
<v Lotan>We are talking about that almost in each minute of the day. Even right now, before you called, we were talking about what we're going to do.
<v Lotan>我们几乎在一天中的每一分钟都在谈论这个。甚至就在你打电话来之前，我们还在讨论我们要怎么做。

00:10:17.480 --> 00:10:35.630
<v Ira Glass>This is Lotan Pinyan. What he's talking about with his wife Michal is, where are they going to live next? They're a youngish couple in Israel with three kids-- 9, 11, and 13 at the time of this conversation. Both educators, he's head of development, building a school and a campus for Bedouins, nomadic Arab tribes. And Michal--
<v Ira Glass>这是洛坦·皮尼安。他和妻子米哈尔正在讨论的是：他们接下来要住在哪里？他们在以色列是一个较为年轻的夫妇，有三个孩子——在这次谈话时分别 9 岁、11 岁和 13 岁。两人都是教育工作者，他是发展部负责人，正在为贝都因人（游牧阿拉伯部落）建立一所学校和一个校园。而米哈尔——

00:10:35.630 --> 00:10:37.710
<v Michal>I'm an educational counselor.
<v Michal>我是一名教育顾问。

00:10:37.710 --> 00:10:44.260
<v Lotan>No, she's a doctor of education. OK? She's a doctor of education.
<v Lotan>不，她是教育学博士。好吗？她是教育学博士。

00:10:44.260 --> 00:10:47.790
<v Ira Glass>Lotan, why do you feel like you have to jump in and say that?
<v Ira Glass>洛坦，你为什么觉得必须插话并说出这一点？

00:10:47.790 --> 00:10:56.970
<v Lotan>Because we can end the conversation without you to know that she's really a doctor.
<v Lotan>因为如果不让你知道她真的是个博士，我们的对话就结束不了了。

00:10:56.970 --> 00:11:14.550
<v Ira Glass>Lotan is, perhaps you can tell, somebody who's always quick to speak his mind, sure of what he thinks. Michal's more deliberative. They used to live in a kibbutz in Israel, Kibbutz Be'eri on the border with Gaza. When Hamas crossed that border on October 7, like so many families, they hid in the safe room in their house.
<v Ira Glass>正如你可能看出来的，洛坦是一个总是直言不讳、对自己的想法深信不疑的人。米哈尔则更有思辨精神。他们过去住在以色列的一个基布兹（集体农庄），即靠近加沙边境的贝埃里基布兹。当哈马斯在 10 月 7 日跨越边界时，和许多家庭一样，他们躲在自己家的安全屋里。

00:11:14.550 --> 00:11:31.980
<v Ira Glass>Dozens of their neighbors were killed, more at Be'eri than at any kibbutz that day-- 1 in 10 people, including Michal's parents. And when it was over, they and most everyone else from the kibbutz were relocated to a tourist hotel by the Dead Sea, an hour-and-a-half drive from their old home in the Gaza border.
<v Ira Glass>他们的数十名邻居被杀，贝埃里那天遇难的人数比任何其他基布兹都多——每 10 人中就有 1 人，其中包括米哈尔的父母。这一切结束后，他们和基布兹的大多数人一起被重新安置到死海边的一个旅游酒店里，距离他们在加沙边境的旧家有一小时半的车程。

00:11:31.980 --> 00:11:39.240
<v Michal>The whole kibbutz is here. The hotel is closed just for our kibbutz. There's no guests from outside.
<v Michal>整个基布兹都在这里。这家酒店只为我们基布兹关闭。没有外面的客人。

00:11:39.240 --> 00:11:50.100
<v Lotan>We are talking about something like 1,000 people inside this hotel. You have a lot of discussions just in the lobby.
<v Lotan>我们谈论的是这家酒店里大约有 1,000 人。光是在大厅里你就会有很多讨论。

00:11:50.100 --> 00:12:04.050
<v Ira Glass>Discussions about where they're all going to live next. In December when we first talked, it was just two months after the attack. And each family had to decide, would they stay with the community? Would they ever want to return to the kibbutz where they'd just gone through this traumatic assault?
<v Ira Glass>关于他们接下来都要住在哪里的讨论。去年十二月我们第一次交谈时，距离袭击发生才刚刚两个月。每个家庭都必须决定：他们会和社区待在一起吗？他们还会想回到那个他们刚刚经历过这场创伤性袭击的基布兹吗？

00:12:04.050 --> 00:12:16.470
<v Lotan>It's 1,000 people. You have a lot of ideas. You have those people who want to stay together wherever the kibbutz will go, and those who say no.
<v Lotan>那是 1,000 个人。你会有很多想法。有些人无论基布兹去哪里都想聚在一起，而有些人则说不。

00:12:16.470 --> 00:12:30.640
<v Ira Glass>Lots of people, especially the older people, were sure they wanted to go back and rebuild. People with kids were all over the place. And leaving a kibbutz, quitting kibbutz life, is a big deal. A kibbutz is a special kind of tight-knit community.
<v Ira Glass>很多人，尤其是老年人，确信他们想回去重建。有孩子的家庭则众说纷纭。离开基布兹，退出基布兹生活，可是一件大事。基布兹是一种特殊的、紧密团结的社区。

00:12:30.640 --> 00:12:47.830
<v Ira Glass>Kibbutzim were originally set up as collective farms on socialist principles, and Be'eri is still run on the old-school socialist ideals most kibbutzim in Israel have abandoned. For example, as a member of the kibbutz, you turn over every cent that you earn at your job, your entire paycheck, to the collective.
<v Ira Glass>基布兹最初是根据社会主义原则建立的集体农庄，而贝埃里至今仍按照以色列大多数基布兹已经抛弃的传统社会主义理想运行。例如，作为基布兹的成员，你必须把你工作赚的每一分钱——你的全部薪水——都交公。

00:12:47.830 --> 00:12:52.220
<v Michal>But the kibbutz gives us everything we need.
<v Michal>但基布兹给我们提供我们需要的一切。

00:12:52.220 --> 00:13:16.810
<v Ira Glass>And that means everything. They give you a house to live in, cars to drive, full health care, a gym and a swimming pool, and a dining hall, if you don't want to cook, and spending money for necessities but also for fun stuff like family vacations, to cover your kids' school and college. Children spend all day every day with other kids their age. It's a great way to raise kids. They can roam around. Michal describes what's so nice about kibbutz life this way.
<v Ira Glass>这意味著一切。他们给你提供房子住、车子开、完善的医疗保险、健身房、游泳池，如果你不想做饭还有食堂，并且提供零花钱用来购买必需品，以及支付家庭度假等娱乐开销，甚至还负责你孩子的学校和大学费用。孩子们整天都和同龄的其他孩子在一起。这是抚养孩子的好方法。他们可以到处跑。米沙尔这样描述基布兹生活的妙处。

00:13:16.810 --> 00:13:31.060
<v Michal>You don't have any worries-- you know, mortgage or payments. And you have a very strong community that makes you feel you belong to something big.
<v Michal>你没有任何忧虑——你知道的，不用担心房贷或各种账单。而且你拥有一个非常强大的社区，让你觉得你属于某种伟大的事物。

00:13:31.060 --> 00:13:48.980
<v Ira Glass>Michal grew up this way on this kibbutz and loved it. Lotan moved to Be'eri with her when they first got together. He chafed at some of the rules at first, but got used to life in this big extended family. Now, after October 7, here he was with all of them at this hotel, figuring out what to do next.
<v Ira Glass>米沙尔就是在这个基布兹长大并深爱着这里的。罗坦最初和她在一起时搬到了贝埃里。一开始他对这里的某些规矩感到有些不习惯，但后来也习惯了这个大家庭的生活。现在，经历过10月7日之后，他和大家一起来到了这家酒店，琢磨着下一步该怎么办。

00:13:48.980 --> 00:14:03.140
<v Lotan>And you know, it's a hotel. It's a nice place for three days of vacation, but it's not a way of living. You know, to see the smallest one go to sleep at something like midnight and--
<v Lotan>你知道的，这只是一家酒店。度假呆三天是个好地方，但这不是一种生活方式。你看，看着最小的孩子半夜左右才去睡觉，还有——

00:14:03.140 --> 00:14:07.720
<v Michal>You don't have a family environment.
<v Michal>你没有家庭环境。

00:14:07.720 --> 00:14:17.330
<v Ira Glass>Michal and Lotan were in one hotel room. The three kids were cooped up in another, getting on each other's nerves and into each other's hair. It's hard to be a family in that scattered situation.
<v Ira Glass>米沙尔和罗坦挤在一个酒店房间里。三个孩子则被关在另一个房间里，互相惹恼，争吵不休。在这种分散的状况下，很难维持一个家庭的样子。

00:14:17.330 --> 00:14:31.080
<v Ira Glass>It was hard to supervise the kids. They were gone all day at work. Michal now had a 90-minute commute each way to and from her job. There was school for the kids, but it was a makeshift thing the kibbutz organized, not real school.
<v Ira Glass>很难照顾孩子们。他们整天都要出去工作。米沙尔现在上下班单程通勤需要90分钟。孩子们虽然有学校可去，但这只是基布兹临时组织的一个场所，并不是真正的学校。

00:14:31.080 --> 00:14:33.750
<v Lotan>It's more like babysitting.
<v Lotan>这更像是临时照看孩子。

00:14:33.750 --> 00:14:40.290
<v Michal>The challenge-- the big challenge is for the older children. They don't want to go.
<v Michal>挑战——最大的挑战是对于大一点的孩子。他们不想去。

00:14:40.290 --> 00:14:50.580
<v Ira Glass>After just a few weeks of this, Lotan started to think, this is not good for our children. Maybe we need to get out of here, leave the hotel, leave our friends and family behind.
<v Ira Glass>仅仅经历了几周这样的生活后，罗坦就开始想，这对我们的孩子没有好处。也许我们需要离开这里，离开酒店，把我们的朋友和家人留在身后。

00:14:50.580 --> 00:15:04.200
<v Lotan>I realized that we're going to be stuck here in the Dead Sea for something like six, seven months. It's not a home. Let's do something else. Let's heal our family.
<v Lotan>我意识到我们将在死海这里被困大约六、七个月。这不是一个家。我们做点别的吧。让我们治愈我们的家庭。

00:15:04.200 --> 00:15:09.300
<v Michal>Lotan was pushing to look for another place.
<v Michal>罗坦一直在极力主张寻找另一个地方。

00:15:09.300 --> 00:15:15.240
<v Ira Glass>Michal was not into this idea at all, of moving away from the hotel, from the other kibbutz members.
<v Ira Glass>米沙尔对搬离酒店、离开其他基布兹成员这个想法一点也不感兴趣。

00:15:15.240 --> 00:15:38.020
<v Michal>I understood why he wants to leave, but it was hard for me to leave the people I grew up with. These are the people I know all my life. So it was hard for me to understand that I'm leaving them. For me, I need to get used to the idea. I need time.
<v Michal>我理解他为什么想离开，但对我来说，离开和我一同长大的人是很艰难的。这些人是我一生都认识的人。所以要我明白自己正在离开他们，这对我来说很难。对我而言，我需要时间去习惯这个想法。我需要时间。

00:15:38.020 --> 00:15:42.370
<v Lotan>That's the process with our relationship.
<v Lotan>这就是我们关系中的处理方式。

00:15:42.370 --> 00:15:46.840
<v Michal>He's fast-forward. [LAUGHS] He's-- he adjusts very quickly.
<v Michal>他是快进型的。（笑）他——他适应得非常快。

00:15:46.840 --> 00:15:50.470
<v Lotan>If I'm deciding something, after I do my own thinking--
<v Lotan>如果我决定了什么事，在我自己思考过之后——

00:15:50.470 --> 00:15:51.310
<v Michal>He's very quick.
<v Michal>他动作非常快。

00:15:51.310 --> 00:15:52.630
<v Lotan>--I move forward.
<v Lotan>——我就向前迈进了。

00:15:52.630 --> 00:15:58.870
<v Michal>And I'm a slower person. [LAUGHS] I need time to do things, you know?
<v Michal>而我是个比较慢的人。（笑）我需要时间来做事，你知道吗？

00:15:58.870 --> 00:16:04.930
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:16:04.930 --> 00:16:17.890
<v Ira Glass>So they had to decide if they would move out of the hotel. But there was a bigger question they were going to have to face someday. Should they move back to their kibbutz near the Gaza border? Like, ever?
<v Ira Glass>所以他们必须决定是否搬出酒店。但他们总有一天要面对一个更大的问题。他们应该搬回加沙边境附近的基布兹吗？哪怕以后还有机会？

00:16:17.890 --> 00:16:35.860
<v Ira Glass>At this point, it's unclear how long the war will drag on. Over 1,600 Israelis have died, according to the Israeli military, and over 29,000 Palestinians, according to the Gaza Ministry of Health. Other kibbutzniks-- kibbutz members-- told me that they would only move back if somehow there were peace at the border.
<v Ira Glass>目前尚不清楚这场战争会拖延多久。根据以色列军方的数据，已有超过1600名以色列人死亡；根据加沙卫生部的数据，有超过29000名巴勒斯坦人死亡。其他基布兹成员告诉我，只有在边境实现某种和平的情况下，他们才会搬回去。

00:16:35.860 --> 00:16:54.550
<v Ira Glass>Or, you know, some conceivable path to some peace plan that would seem fair for the Palestinians so the kibbutzniks wouldn't have to worry about future attacks coming across the border, which, in a practical way, probably means they won't be moving back any time soon. Michal and Lotan try to imagine what it would take for them to return.
<v Ira Glass>或者，你知道的，某种针对和平计划的可行途径，对巴勒斯坦人来说显得公正，这样基布兹成员就不必担心未来边境会发生袭击——从实际角度来看，这可能意味着他们不会在短期内搬回去。米沙尔和罗坦试图想象他们要付出什么代价才能回去。

00:16:54.550 --> 00:17:05.440
<v Michal>You know, Lotan was asking me a few days ago, do you want to go to our home? Can you go back to this house?
<v Michal>你知道的，罗坦几天前问我，你想回家吗？你能回到这个房子里吗？

00:17:05.440 --> 00:17:28.000
<v Lotan>Do you want to go back to a graveyard that 92 of your kibbutz members, including your father and mother, murdered there? Do you want to go back? You're going to cross the places that you know what happened there. The stories will come out in front of your eyes. It's a question.
<v Lotan>你想回到一个有92名基布兹成员——包括你的爸爸妈妈——在那里被谋杀的墓地吗？你想回去吗？你要穿过那些你知道曾经发生过那些事的地点。那些故事会浮现在你的眼前。这是一个问题。

00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:40.870
<v Ira Glass>OK, so feel free to say as much or as little about this as you want. I'd like to ask you just a little bit about what happened with your family on October 7, just so people understand what it is you'd be going back to.
<v Ira Glass>好的，对于这件事，你想说多少就说多少。我想问你一点关于你家人在10月7日经历的事情，好让大家明白你们到底要面对的是什么。

00:17:40.870 --> 00:18:08.020
<v Lotan>At 6:30 AM, we had unnormal, unusual, massive missiles attack. And when I say unusual, it's not normal to have even one missile. But that was really a massive attack, so we ran to the safe room, closed the door. And the first thought I had is, OK, it's 6:30 AM. We probably will not go back to sleep. Let's turn on the coffee machine.
<v Lotan>早上6点30分，我们遭到了不正常、不寻常的大规模导弹袭击。当我说不寻常的时候，意思是连一枚导弹都不正常。但那真的是一次大规模袭击，所以我们跑进安全室，关上了门。我脑子里的第一个想法是，好吧，现在是早上6点30分。我们可能没办法回去睡觉了。把咖啡机打开吧。

00:18:08.020 --> 00:18:24.050
<v Ira Glass>For decades, safe rooms have been required in all new Israeli homes, reinforced to withstand rocket attacks. Soon, Michal and Lotan see on the kibbutz WhatsApp group that attackers have made it inside the kibbutz. But they assume it's small, maybe 5 or 10 fighters, and they assume the army will be there soon.
<v Ira Glass>几十年来，以色列所有新住宅都强制要求建造安全室，并经过加固以抵御火箭弹袭击。很快，米沙尔和罗坦在基布兹的WhatsApp群里看到袭击者已经进入了基布兹。但他们以为人数不多，可能只有5到10名武装人员，并且他们以为军队很快就会赶到。

00:18:24.050 --> 00:18:53.000
<v Lotan>But minutes after that, when everyone was saying they are here, they are there, they are shooting at my house, they are throwing grenades, and you know everyone was leaving, you understand that they are spread all over the kibbutz. And then, just close the door and try to find ropes and things like that in order to tight the handle of the door because people were trying to-- were starting to write that they are seeking to open the safe room and taking people outside and shoot them.
<v Lotan>但在那几分钟之后，当每个人都在说他们在那里、他们在哪里、他们正在向我的房子射击、他们正在扔手榴弹，而且你知道每个人都在离开时，你才明白他们已经散布在整个基布兹。然后，只能关上门，试着找绳子之类的东西把门把手绑紧，因为人们开始写消息说袭击者正试图打开安全室，把人拉到外面去枪杀。

00:18:53.000 --> 00:19:00.020
<v Ira Glass>We should explain that the safe rooms are designed for missiles coming in, not for people coming in trying to break into the safe room, so they don't lock from the inside.
<v Ira Glass>我们应该解释一下，安全室是为了抵御飞来的导弹而设计的，而不是为了防范试图闯入安全室的人，所以它们不能从里面反锁。

00:19:00.020 --> 00:19:01.540
<v Michal>Exactly.
<v Michal>没错。

00:19:01.540 --> 00:19:26.770
<v Lotan>Exactly because that's one huge part of our family story. Because something like 9:15, Michal's mother was writing us on the WhatsApp, there are terrorists near our house, and then there are terrorists inside the house. They are throwing grenades. They are shooting through the safe room door. And--
<v Lotan>没错，因为这是我们家族故事中极其沉重的一部分。大约9点15分时，米沙尔的母亲在WhatsApp上给我们留言，说我们家附近有恐怖分子，然后说恐怖分子已经进屋了。他们在扔手榴弹。他们在透过安全室的门开枪。然后——

00:19:26.770 --> 00:19:42.150
<v Michal>We didn't understand that my dad was shot while sitting in the safe room. The bullets penetrate the door, and he was wounded while sitting in the safe room.
<v Michal>我们当时并不知道我爸爸在安全室里坐着的时候中枪了。子弹穿透了门，他在安全室里坐着时受了伤。

00:19:42.150 --> 00:19:45.000
<v Ira Glass>Michal's brother Yuval talked to their mom then.
<v Ira Glass>米沙尔的哥哥尤瓦尔当时和他们的妈妈通了话。

00:19:45.000 --> 00:20:09.230
<v Lotan>He was telling her, Mom, be strong. Keep the door tight. The army is on its way. He was lying. There is no army at the kibbutz. But she-- that she was a really strong, realistic woman. She was saying to him, no, Yuval, I think this is the last time we're going to talk. Please tell everybody that we love them.
<v Lotan>他对她说，妈妈，坚强点。把门拉紧。军队在路上了。他在撒谎。基布兹根本没有军队。但她——她是一个非常坚强、现实的女人。她对他说，不，尤瓦尔，我想这是我们最后一次谈话了。请告诉所有人我们爱他们。

00:20:09.230 --> 00:20:24.010
<v Ira Glass>The last messages on WhatsApp have Michal and her brothers saying, "Mom, keep writing all the time. What's happening with you? Mom, answer." Their mom says, "Dad was shot, and they're throwing grenades. They blew up the safe room."
<v Ira Glass>WhatsApp上的最后几条消息是米沙尔和她的兄弟们在说：“妈妈，一直发消息。你们怎么样了？妈妈，回答。”他们的妈妈说：“爸爸中枪了，他们在扔手榴弹。他们把安全室炸了。”

00:20:24.010 --> 00:20:49.600
<v Lotan>And at 10:04, she was writing on the WhatsApp, "help, help," twice, and that's the last word we have. And now, imagine that we are sitting in our safe room, one-minute walk in from them, can do nothing, and realize that we don't have any more mom or dad for Michal, grandfather and grandmother for our children.
<v Lotan>在10点04分，她在WhatsApp上连续发了两次“救命，救命”，那是我们收到的最后的话。现在，想象一下，我们坐在离他们只有一分钟步行路程的安全室里，什么也做不了，并且意识到米沙尔再也没有爸爸妈妈了，我们的孩子再也没有爷爷奶奶了。

00:20:49.600 --> 00:20:51.800
<v Ira Glass>Do you tell the children?
<v Ira Glass>你们告诉孩子们了吗？

00:20:51.800 --> 00:21:14.150
<v Michal>Yes, they heard. They knew. They kept asking, what about Grandpa and Grandma? And the only answer I had was, I don't know. I didn't want to say something that is not true. So I just told them that I don't know. It's hard to hear that-- I don't know.
<v Michal>是的，他们听到了。他们知道了。他们一直在问，爷爷奶奶怎么样了？我唯一的回答是，我不知道。我不想说不是实话的话。所以我只能告诉他们我不知道。听到这句话很难受——我不知道。

00:21:14.150 --> 00:21:19.380
<v Ira Glass>Lotan says at this point they realized they needed to do anything they could think of to keep their kids alive.
<v Ira Glass>罗坦说，当时他们意识到他们需要做任何能想到的事情来让孩子们活下来。

00:21:19.380 --> 00:21:32.090
<v Lotan>But what I had only is a baseball bat, a dog that probably would got killed the first time they're going to go, and they were hanging under our house.
<v Lotan>但我手里只有一根棒球棒，还有一只狗——如果他们出去的话，这只狗很可能会在第一时间被杀死，而那些人当时就悬在我们房子下方。

00:21:32.090 --> 00:21:44.090
<v Ira Glass>They-- Hamas fighters-- seemed like they were using the storage space downstairs as a small base, keeping weapons there. The family heard explosions and gunfire. The youngest, the boy again, is nine years old.
<v Ira Glass>他们——哈马斯武装分子——似乎把楼下的储藏空间当作了一个小型基地，并在那里存放武器。一家人听到了爆炸声和枪声。最小的那个，也就是那个男孩，今年九岁。

00:21:44.090 --> 00:22:23.970
<v Lotan>And my son, in a terrified way, told us, Dad, Dad, I don't want to die here. And we look at him and told everyone, no, no one is going to die here. We have the dog, the baseball bat, and we are in the second floor. That's our fortress. And you, all of you, are now going to write what you're going to take when we're going to be outside, when they're going to rescue us. And they made a list.
<v Lotan>我儿子带着恐惧对我们说，爸爸，爸爸，我不想死在这里。我们看着他，对所有人说，不，没有人会死在这里。我们有狗，有棒球棒，而且我们在二楼。这就是我们的堡垒。而你们，所有人，现在要把当我们出去、当他们来救援我们时你们要带的东西写下来。然后他们列了一张清单。

00:22:23.970 --> 00:22:40.740
<v Ira Glass>And they waited, from the morning all through the afternoon and into the night, trying not to make any noise at all. They had no food, no water. If someone had to pee, they did it in a wastebasket while everybody looked away. They were there for 19 hours with their kids.
<v Ira Glass>他们从清晨一直等到了下午，又熬到了深夜，尽量不发出任何声音。他们没有食物，没有水。如果有人必须小便，就尿在废纸篓里，同时大家把头转开。他们和孩子在那里待了19个小时。

00:22:40.740 --> 00:22:43.600
<v Ira Glass>What did they do all that time?
<v Ira Glass>他们这一整天都在做什么？

00:22:43.600 --> 00:22:44.100
<v Michal>Nothing.
<v Michal>什么都没做。

00:22:43.960 --> 00:22:46.540
<v Lotan>They didn't do nothing.
<v Lotan>他们什么都没做。

00:22:46.540 --> 00:22:48.140
<v Michal>We did nothing.
<v Michal>我们什么都没做。

00:22:48.140 --> 00:23:17.460
<v Lotan>They watched me and woke me up if I was falling asleep because I was handling the handle of the door with a baseball bat and what I ever did to tight the door with ropes and things like that. They heard the shooting or the explosion or anything like that, they just were silenced. They were amazing. They understand the situation.
<v Lotan>如果我快要睡着了，他们会看着我并把我叫醒，因为我要用棒球棒死死抵住门把手，并用我所能找到的绳子和其他东西把门绑紧。他们听到枪声、爆炸声或任何类似的声音时，都只是保持沉默。他们太棒了。他们理解当时的处境。

00:23:17.460 --> 00:23:28.430
<v Ira Glass>Finally, the army came at midnight and rescued them. When they got to the hotel the next day, of course the kids couldn't just shake this off. Their son, the nine-year-old, had had a full day of whispering in the safe room.
<v Ira Glass>最后，军队在午夜赶来救出了他们。第二天他们到达酒店时，孩子们当然无法轻易摆脱这种阴影。他们9岁的儿子在安全室里度过了整整一天的小声耳语。

00:23:28.430 --> 00:23:56.900
<v Lotan>He's the most, I think, the fragile one. And he kept whispering three days after that in the hotel. And we just brought him his drums and his LEGO, and he started to live again, so it's OK.
<v Lotan>我觉得他是最脆弱的一个。在酒店的那三天里，他一直都在小声说话。我们只是把他的架子鼓和乐高积木拿给他，他又开始恢复生活了，所以没关系。

00:23:56.900 --> 00:24:07.040
<v Ira Glass>So could they ever go back and live at Be'eri? Two or three weeks after the attack, Michal went home again, the first time, to the kibbutz to see what it was like.
<v Ira Glass>那么他们还能回去住在贝埃里吗？袭击发生两三周后，米沙尔再次回到家，这是她第一次回到基布兹看看那里是什么样子。

00:24:07.040 --> 00:24:21.650
<v Michal>Going back was very hard for me. When I was there, I just shut myself. So I just saw everything, but I was very-- how do you say this word? I don't know. [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]
<v Michal>回去对我来说非常艰难。我在那里的时候，整个人都封闭了。我看到了所有东西，但我非常——这个词怎么说来着？我不知道。（外语讲话）

00:24:21.650 --> 00:24:22.160
<v Lotan>Very cool.
<v Lotan>非常冷漠。

00:24:22.160 --> 00:24:24.380
<v Michal>I was very cool, yes.
<v Michal>我非常冷漠，是的。

00:24:24.380 --> 00:24:26.430
<v Ira Glass>Like your feelings were shut down.
<v Ira Glass>就像你的情感被关闭了一样。

00:24:26.430 --> 00:24:27.320
<v Michal>Exactly.
<v Michal>没错。

00:24:27.320 --> 00:24:27.820
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:24:27.710 --> 00:24:44.780
<v Michal>I was there for, like, 10 minutes. I took some stuff that my daughter asked. And just, I couldn't stand-- it's not my home. It was so messy and dirty. And I couldn't stay there, so--
<v Michal>我在那里待了大约10分钟。我拿了一些女儿要的东西。我实在无法忍受——那不是我的家。那里太乱太脏了。我无法留在那里，所以——

00:24:44.780 --> 00:24:48.560
<v Ira Glass>That's interesting. It didn't even seem like your home. It just seemed like some other place?
<v Ira Glass>这很有意思。它甚至看起来不像你的家了？只像是别的什么地方？

00:24:48.560 --> 00:24:52.300
<v Michal>No. No. It's not mine.
<v Michal>不。不。那不是我的。

00:24:52.300 --> 00:25:09.970
<v Ira Glass>Other people I talked to who returned to their kibbutzim told me that walking into their homes was like everything was frozen from when they ran out, like it was still October 7. One woman said she felt like she was watching herself in a movie. She felt so distanced from it. One man told me he tries to go back once a week to try to absorb what's happened.
<v Ira Glass>我采访过的其他回到基布兹的人告诉我，走进他们的家，感觉一切都定格在他们逃跑的那一刻，就像时间还停留在10月7日。一位女士说她觉得自己像是在看一部电影里的自己。她感觉与这一切极其疏离。一位男士告诉我，他努力争取每周回去一次，以试图消化所发生的一切。

00:25:09.970 --> 00:25:18.690
<v Michal>I couldn't even sit on my sofa. I was there just for 10 minutes. I couldn't stay in the house.
<v Michal>我甚至无法坐在我的沙发上。我在那儿只待了10分钟。我无法待在屋里。

00:25:18.690 --> 00:25:25.160
<v Ira Glass>So when you think about going back there, what do you think? Going back there to try to live there, how would that work?
<v Ira Glass>所以当你想起要回到那里时，你是怎么想的？回到那里试图生活下去，那会是怎样的情形？

00:25:25.160 --> 00:25:54.200
<v Michal>I don't know. I don't know. That's the big question. Some days, I'm telling to myself, yes, I want to go back. And some days, I'm telling, what are you thinking about? This is not a place to live. It's a graveyard. No. No, no. It's still not-- it's not a decision I can make now. I need more time.
<v Michal>我不知道。我不知道。这是个巨大的问题。有时候，我告诉自己，是的，我想回去。而有时候，我又会想，你在胡思乱想什么？这不是一个能生活的地方。这是一片墓地。不。不，不。这仍然不是我能现在做出的决定。我需要更多时间。

00:25:54.200 --> 00:26:04.530
<v Ira Glass>So she went back to the hotel. Where they still had to decide, where would they live right now? Should they leave the hotel and set out on their own in the short run?
<v Ira Glass>于是她回到了酒店。在那里，他们仍然必须做出决定：他们现在究竟该住在哪里？他们应该离开酒店，在短期内靠自己重新开始吗？

00:26:04.530 --> 00:26:20.310
<v Ira Glass>By early November, Lotan definitely wanted to leave. Michal did not. And then she saw how badly their oldest daughter, Aya, was doing. Again, Aya's 13. And in the weeks after October 7, she was the person in the family struggling the most.
<v Ira Glass>到了11月初，罗坦坚决想要离开。米沙尔则不想。接着，她看到了他们的大女儿阿亚的情况有多糟糕。再说一次，阿亚13岁。在10月7日之后的几周里，她是家里挣扎得最厉害的人。

00:26:20.310 --> 00:26:30.990
<v Ira Glass>She was skipping school every day and tried to hide it from them. She just stayed in her room all day, doing nothing. This was completely unlike her, Michal says.
<v Ira Glass>她每天都逃学，并试图瞒着他们。她整天待在房间里，什么也不做。米沙尔说，这跟她以前完全判若两人。

00:26:30.990 --> 00:26:41.490
<v Ira Glass>Before October 7, she loved going to school, went happily, never missed a day. She was an athlete, came home from volleyball at 7:00 three nights a week. At night, she would hang with her friends. She was busy.
<v Ira Glass>10月7日之前，她喜欢上学，总是开开心心地去，从来没有缺课过。她是一名运动员，一周有三个晚上7点打完排球回家。晚上，她会和朋友们在一起。她总是忙个不停。

00:26:41.490 --> 00:27:06.310
<v Michal>To see her going to sleep very late at night and wanting to sleep late in the morning-- everything she had is not-- it's not happening. No school, no learning, no volleyball. The only thing that's left is her friends, which are confused as she is.
<v Michal>看到她深夜才睡，早晨又想赖床——她曾经拥有的一切都不复存在了。没有学校，没有学习，没有排球。唯一剩下的只有她的朋友们，而他们也和她一样感到困惑。

00:27:06.310 --> 00:27:32.010
<v Michal>So they're going around with no purpose. And this is what bothers us the most, that there is no purpose for them here. They need something to wake up for in the morning. And over here, it's too much pain and too much dark.
<v Michal>所以他们无所事事地四处游荡。而这正是最令我们困扰的地方，他们在那里毫无生活目标。他们需要早晨醒来时有所期待。而在这里，痛苦太多，黑暗太多。

00:27:32.010 --> 00:27:42.760
<v Ira Glass>Seeing Aya like this, that's what convinced Michal that Lotan was right. They needed to get away from the hotel. Their kids needed that. But where to go?
<v Ira Glass>看到阿亚变成这样，这让米沙尔确信罗坦是对的。他们必须离开酒店。他们的孩子需要这样。但能去哪儿呢？

00:27:42.760 --> 00:28:02.290
<v Ira Glass>Now, as they were talking about this as a couple, members of the kibbutz were in a much bigger conversation about leaving the hotel as a group. There was so much damage to their kibbutz, so many homes and buildings set on fire, burned to the ground, bombed, hit with grenades and bullets. It would take two or three years to rebuild before they could move back.
<v Ira Glass>现在，正当他们作为夫妻讨论这件事时，基布兹的成员们正在进行一场规模大得多的讨论，商量作为整体离开酒店。他们的基布兹遭到严重破坏，那么多房屋和建筑被纵火、烧为平地、被炸毁、遭到手榴弹和子弹袭击。要花两三年的时间重建，他们才能搬回来。

00:28:02.290 --> 00:28:18.070
<v Ira Glass>So they were looking at temporary places that the whole group could relocate to while that happened. Lotan was one of the kibbutz leaders figuring out where they should go. The choice came down to two locations, and Lotan had decided that wanted to go to a peaceful village called Kedma.
<v Ira Glass>因此，他们正在寻找临时住所，以便在重建期间整个群体可以集体搬迁过去。罗坦是决定他们该去哪里的基布兹领导人之一。选择最终缩小到两个地点，而罗坦决定去一个名为基德玛（Kedma）的平和村庄。

00:28:18.070 --> 00:28:39.580
<v Ira Glass>The idea is, everybody could move there and heal, figure out in a few years whether they will move back to Be'eri or not. The vote was November 22. Lotan's side lost by 40 votes. And the place the kibbutz chose-- in Hatzerim, near a big airbase-- that was a place that he and Michal did not want to live.
<v Ira Glass>这个想法是，每个人都可以搬到那里疗伤，并在几年内弄清楚他们是否会搬回贝埃里。投票在11月22日举行。罗坦的一派以40票落败。而基布兹选择的地方——位于哈齐里姆（Hatzerim），靠近一个大型空军基地——那是他和米沙尔并不想去居住的地方。

00:28:39.580 --> 00:28:45.640
<v Lotan>I knew already. I knew for sure that she's thinking the same.
<v Lotan>我当时就知道了。我确信她也有同感。

00:28:45.640 --> 00:28:52.360
<v Michal>Yeah. I remember he told me, I'm not moving to Hatzerim. And I said, OK.
<v Michal>对。我记得他说过，我才不搬到哈泽里姆。我说，好吧。

00:28:52.360 --> 00:29:01.020
<v Ira Glass>And that decided it. They were not going to stay with the kibbutz. They'd strike out on their own. So they talked to their kids about this plan.
<v Ira Glass>事情就这么决定了。他们不打算留在基布兹。他们要独立闯出一片天。所以他们和孩子们谈了谈这个计划。

00:29:01.020 --> 00:29:15.130
<v Ira Glass>Aya hated it. She didn't want to speak with me for this story, but her parents say she was angry. One thing she still had from her old life was her friends, who had just gone through this trauma together-- were still going through it, in a way.
<v Ira Glass>阿亚恨透了。她不愿意为了这个故事跟我说话，但她父母说她很生气。她从老生活里唯一保留下来的东西就是她的朋友们，那些刚一起经历过这场创伤的人——从某种意义上说，还在经历着。

00:29:15.130 --> 00:29:29.390
<v Lotan>You know, she had two friends that were kidnapped, and they returned from Gaza Strip. And she has also one friend that was severely wounded and lost her mother and a brother. So--
<v Lotan>你知道，她有两个朋友被绑架了，她们从加沙地带回来了。她还有一个朋友受了重伤，失去了母亲和兄弟。所以——

00:29:29.390 --> 00:29:30.110
<v Michal>One is dead.
<v Michal>有一个死了。

00:29:30.110 --> 00:29:31.460
<v Lotan>And one is dead.
<v Lotan>还有一个死了。

00:29:31.460 --> 00:29:36.170
<v Ira Glass>And what did she say to you when she was angry about it? What was she saying?
<v Ira Glass>当她为此生气时，她对你说了什么？她都在说些什么？

00:29:36.170 --> 00:29:57.110
<v Michal>She was shouting why we have to take her apart of her friends and that we're not thinking about her. It was hard to hear. You know, when you live in the kibbutz, the friends are the most important thing.
<v Michal>她大喊大叫，问我们为什么要让她离开朋友，说我们根本没有为她着想。这很难听。你知道，当你住在基布兹时，朋友就是最重要的事情。

00:29:57.110 --> 00:30:18.350
<v Michal>I don't know how to express this in words. But we have this connection that you cannot find anywhere else because we grew up together. We did everything together. We hang out in the morning, in the afternoon. We slept together. We ate together. We studied together. We did everything together.
<v Michal>我不知道该怎么用言语表达。但我们有那种你在别处绝对找不到的联系，因为我们是一起长大的。我们什么都在一起做。我们早上、下午都在一起闲逛。我们一起睡觉。我们一起吃饭。我们一起学习。我们什么都在一起做。

00:30:18.350 --> 00:30:38.270
<v Michal>And this is what she has right now, and she's going to lose it. I understand why she's angry. I understand. But I don't want that death will be so present in every way.
<v Michal>这就是她现在所拥有的，而她就要失去它了。我理解她为什么生气。我理解。但我不想让死亡在方方面面都如此挥之不去。

00:30:38.270 --> 00:30:39.110
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:30:39.110 --> 00:30:48.700
<v Michal>I don't want it.
<v Michal>我不想这样。

00:30:48.700 --> 00:31:00.070
<v Ira Glass>Their two other kids were good with the idea of them leaving the hotel. The littlest told them, we can live in a car as long as we're together. It's fine. But with Aya, she came back to it over and over.
<v Ira Glass>他们的另外两个孩子对离开酒店这个想法挺赞同的。最小的对他们说，只要我们在一起，住在车里也行。没关系的。但对阿亚来说，她一而再、再而三地纠结这件事。

00:31:00.070 --> 00:31:02.060
<v Lotan>It happens all the time.
<v Lotan>这种事随时都在发生。

00:31:02.060 --> 00:31:07.140
<v Michal>It's every day. Are you sure you want to go? Are you sure you're not going to stay?
<v Michal>每天都是这样。你们确定要走吗？你们确定不留下来吗？

00:31:07.140 --> 00:31:29.170
<v Ira Glass>Michal explained to Aya that since they really might never move back to Be'eri, it didn't make sense to move to the temporary location with her friends right now and then split with them in three years and start over again. Easier to leave now, at an age when she can make new friends. She told Aya that she would drive her back to see her old friends any time she wants. But there are certain things she didn't say to Aya.
<v Ira Glass>米哈尔向阿亚解释说，既然他们可能真的永远不会搬回贝埃里了，现在和朋友们一起搬去临时住所，然后在三年后和他们分开、重新开始，这就毫无意义了。不如现在就离开，趁着还能结交新朋友的年纪。她告诉阿亚，只要她想，她随时可以开车带她回去看老朋友。但有些话她没有对阿亚说。

00:31:29.170 --> 00:31:40.450
<v Michal>I didn't tell her that I don't want to live in a place that mourning is going to be a big part of everything.
<v Michal>我没有告诉她，我不想生活在一个哀悼将成为一切中重要一部分的地方。

00:31:40.450 --> 00:31:41.920
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:31:41.920 --> 00:31:44.710
<v Michal>I cannot tell her this.
<v Michal>我不能告诉她这个。

00:31:44.710 --> 00:31:46.330
<v Ira Glass>Why not?
<v Ira Glass>为什么不呢？

00:31:46.330 --> 00:31:49.400
<v Michal>I don't know. I don't know.
<v Michal>我不知道。我不知道。

00:31:49.400 --> 00:31:55.640
<v Ira Glass>Do you not want to say that to her because you're scared it'll affect how she sees her friends and their life back in the kibbutz?
<v Ira Glass>你不想对她说这些，是因为你害怕这会影响她对朋友以及他们在基布兹的生活的看法吗？

00:31:55.640 --> 00:32:12.290
<v Michal>Yes. You know, just yesterday they told us that Yossi, her best friend's father, were murdered in captivity. So it's going to happen all the time.
<v Michal>是的。你知道，就在昨天他们告诉我们，她最好朋友的父亲约西在被囚禁期间被谋杀 了。所以这种事会一直发生。

00:32:12.290 --> 00:32:27.010
<v Ira Glass>That conversation was in January. They were still in the hotel. The plan was to move February 1 to a house near a city called Kiryat Gat, maybe a half-hour drive from the border. And it's at this point the family got a lucky break-- something they didn't expect.
<v Ira Glass>那次对话是在一月份。他们当时还在酒店。计划是二月一日搬到靠近基尔雅特加特的一个小镇附近的一栋房子里，距离边境大概半小时车程。正是在这个时候，这家人迎来了一个幸运的突破——一个他们意料之外的转机。

00:32:27.010 --> 00:32:35.980
<v Michal>And the school she's going to go to, there is friends that she knows from our area that moved to the same place.
<v Michal>而且她要去的学校里，有她认识的、从我们地区搬到同一个地方的朋友。

00:32:35.980 --> 00:32:39.190
<v Ira Glass>Oh, wow. That's really, really fortunate.
<v Ira Glass>哦，哇。那真的、真的很幸运。

00:32:39.190 --> 00:32:53.990
<v Michal>Yes. So it makes her heart more calm. And these are kids that she knows and she's friends of, not from Be'eri exactly, but just from our area.
<v Michal>是的。所以这让她的心情平静了下来。这些都是她认识并交好的孩子，虽然不是完全来自贝埃里，但也是来自我们地区。

00:32:53.990 --> 00:33:08.170
<v Michal>So she's not opposite anymore, as she used to. Today was the first day she went to school and studied and opened her notebook. Just today, after three months.
<v Michal>所以她不再像以前那样抗拒了。今天她第一天去学校上学、学习并打开了笔记本。就在今天，三个月之后。

00:33:08.170 --> 00:33:27.020
<v Ira Glass>Maybe, Michal said, she finally understands that things are going back to normal and she's going to have to go to school like normal with other kids who are moving on.
<v Ira Glass>米哈尔说，也许她终于明白，生活正在恢复正常，她将不得不像正常人一样去上学，和那些正在继续前行的其他孩子在一起。

00:33:27.020 --> 00:33:33.560
<v Ira Glass>As move-in day approached, feelings in the family were all over the place. Aya was up and down-- a lot of down.
<v Ira Glass>随着入住日期的临近，家里的情绪五味杂陈。阿亚的情绪起伏不定——消极的时候居多。

00:33:33.560 --> 00:33:58.700
<v Michal>I think Abel and Dedar, the little kids, they are excited to see the new life. Lotan is very excited about this. I'm not excited. I feel sad leaving the kibbutz. And even though I decided to do that, it's still sad for me.
<v Michal>我想阿贝尔和德达，那两个小家伙，他们对看到新生活感到兴奋。洛坦对这个非常兴奋。我不兴奋。离开基布兹让我感到难过。尽管这是我决定的，但我还是觉得很难过。

00:33:58.700 --> 00:34:12.390
<v Michal>This is our decision of moving, but I didn't choose it. This is what makes me so sad. They chose it. They decided it for me.
<v Michal>这是我们搬家的决定，但我并没有选择它。这就是让我如此难过的原因。是他们选择了它。是他们替我决定的。

00:34:12.390 --> 00:34:14.990
<v Ira Glass>"They." You mean Hamas.
<v Ira Glass>“他们。”你是说哈马斯。

00:34:14.990 --> 00:34:16.580
<v Michal>Yes.
<v Michal>是的。

00:34:16.580 --> 00:34:18.720
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:34:18.720 --> 00:34:26.370
<v Michal>And it's hard for me not to have my parents around and not to ask them what they think.
<v Michal>而且我很难过身边没有父母，没法问他们的想法。

00:34:26.370 --> 00:34:28.750
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:34:28.750 --> 00:34:48.880
<v Michal>And you know, my mom, she was-- if I would move, she would come and help me clean and help me decide what to do. And now, she's not here to do that. So every decision is-- she's so present with her not-presence. (CHUCKLING) I don't know how to say it.
<v Michal>你知道，我妈妈，她以前——如果我要搬家，她会来帮我打扫卫生，帮我决定该怎么做。而现在，她不在身边做这些了。所以每一个决定都是——她的缺席感是如此强烈。（轻笑）我不知道该怎么表达。

00:34:48.880 --> 00:34:56.630
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:34:56.630 --> 00:35:13.280
<v Ira Glass>They moved February 1, and I called one last time a few weeks after that. The kids are in their new schools. The family's renting a house. Lotan and Michal are bracing for what it's going to be like to have their paychecks paid to them directly and living the way you and I do, where we have to worry about money and paying bills.
<v Ira Glass>他们在二月一日搬了家，几周后我最后一次打电话过去。孩子们都在新学校上学。这家人租了一栋房子。洛坦和米哈尔正在做心理准备，迎接薪水直接打到卡里、过着你我那样必须为金钱和付账单操心的生活。

00:35:13.280 --> 00:35:31.770
<v Ira Glass>But one thing that was different in this last call was that they both seemed way more certain that they're never going to move back to Be'eri. There's a possibility, sure, but they both said it's likely they'll never return. Aya, meanwhile, is mad all the time. It's very severe, Michal told me, worse than before.
<v Ira Glass>但最后这通电话里有一点不同，那就是他们俩似乎都更加确定自己再也不会搬回贝埃里了。当然有这种可能性，但他们都说大概率再也不会回去了。与此同时，阿亚一直在发脾气。米哈尔告诉我，情况非常严重，比以前更糟了。

00:35:31.770 --> 00:36:02.450
<v Ira Glass>She asks all the time to be driven back to the hotel to see her friends. She's gone back once a week. Michal feels like this is one of these parenting choices where she and Lotan made the right call to get the family away from the hotel and start a new life. But that doesn't make it feel any better. She told me she hopes that someday Aya will understand and even forgive them for it.
<v Ira Glass>她总是要求开车送她回酒店去看朋友。她已经回去过一次了。米哈尔觉得这就像是那种育儿抉择，她和洛坦做出了正确的决定，把家人带离酒店并开启新生活。但这并没有让感觉好受一点。她告诉我，她希望有一天阿亚能够理解甚至原谅他们。

00:36:02.450 --> 00:36:19.200
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, if you want to inspire your family at a family meeting once a week every single week, after a while, how do you come up with anything original to say? That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，如果你想在每周一次的家庭会议上激励你的家人，周复一周，过了一段时间后，你该怎么想出任何有创意的话来呢？稍后芝加哥公共广播电台将继续播出我们的节目。

00:36:19.200 --> 00:36:31.710
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's show, "Family Meeting," the stories of people who care about each other, or are supposed to care about each other, coming together to figure stuff out. And of course, these sort of meetings happen in non-family settings also.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的节目是“家庭会议”，讲述了那些关心彼此、或者本该关心彼此的人们聚在一起解决问题的故事。当然，这种会议也会在非家庭的环境中发生。

00:36:31.710 --> 00:36:48.750
<v Ira Glass>I personally find it revolting when any boss refers to his employees as family, but I acknowledge there is an entire workplace version of this. And of course, there's a whole tradition of this in sports-- high school sports teams, where the coach sits everybody down for the big "come to Jesus" talk about how things have been going and what needs to happen next.
<v Ira Glass>我个人觉得，当任何老板把他的员工称为家人时，我都感到反感，但我承认，整个职场都有这种说法。当然，体育界也有这种传统——高中体育球队里，教练把每个人召集起来，开诚布公地谈谈最近的情况以及接下来需要做些什么。

00:36:48.750 --> 00:37:07.050
<v Ira Glass>One of our producers, Chris Benderev, was looking around at the stuff that high school coaches say to teams before their big games. He found a lot of "let's get out there and crush them." Also a lot of the underdog version of that-- "nobody believes in us, but I believe in us." And then he ran across this one speech where the coach tried a different approach. Kind of striking.
<v Ira Glass>我们的制作人之一克里斯·本德雷夫（Chris Benderev）观察了高中教练在大赛前对队员们说的话。他发现了很多“冲出去粉碎他们”之类的话。还有很多弱者版本的豪言壮语——“没有人相信我们，但我相信我们”。然后他偶然听到了一场演讲，教练尝试了不同方法。挺震撼的。

00:37:07.050 --> 00:37:11.850
<v McGuire>Talking to most of you seniors. You know, when you go back to--
<v McGuire>对你们大多数高三学生说。你知道，当你回到——

00:37:11.850 --> 00:37:23.830
<v Ira Glass>This is in a locker room in Southlake, Texas. The team is the Cedar High School High Longhorns. Their coach, Joey McGuire. They're in the playoffs. If they lose this game, season's over. The team is ready to go out onto the field.
<v Ira Glass>这是在德克萨斯州南湖的一个更衣室里。这支球队是锡达高中长角牛队。他们的教练是乔伊·麦圭尔。他们正在打季后赛。如果他们输掉这场比赛，赛季就结束了。球队已经准备好走上球场了。

00:37:23.830 --> 00:37:40.750
<v McGuire>You know, this is a lot different. You go week five, week six, there's always another week seven. This is different. If we don't take care of business, that's it. Ain't coming back in the locker room next week.
<v McGuire>你知道，这很不一样。你打第五周、第六周时，总还会有第七周。这次不同。如果我们不把事情办妥，那就彻底结束了。下周不会再回到这个更衣室了。

00:37:40.750 --> 00:37:54.670
<v McGuire>(SHOUTING) If you care about each other enough like we talk about, if you care about and love each other like we talk about, you have a sense of urgency to get one more week with your family, to get one more week with your family! Go find that--
<v McGuire>(大喊) 如果你们像我们所说的那样在乎彼此，如果你们像我们所说的那样关心和爱护彼此，你们就会有一种紧迫感，为了能和你们的家人多过一周，为了能和你们的家人多过一周！去找回那个——

00:37:54.670 --> 00:38:09.520
<v Ira Glass>Smart, right? Win, and we get one more week together. Our producer, Chris, wondered if this was a move that the coach uses a lot, so he called up Coach McGuire, who said, yes, he has said this before in other playoffs. But he says it's also a thing he feels.
<v Ira Glass>因为那是终点。再也没有每周聚在一起的日子了。即使他们赢了，也会有那种失落感。他说他总是特意感谢高三的学生，因为对于高三学生来说，离开这个大家庭往往是最艰难的。

00:38:09.520 --> 00:38:23.410
<v McGuire>I talk all the time about being a family and being more than just a team. And that was such a huge game. I mean, there's probably 15,000 people. It's two of the best teams in the nation.
<v McGuire>第二幕，以绝交告终。有时候，召开家庭会议的原因是孩子在学校遇到了冲突。然后父母就得决定，是我孩子做错了事，还是问题出在另一个孩子身上？

00:38:23.410 --> 00:38:40.680
<v Ira Glass>These are Texas high school teams working their way up to a state championship. Longhorns were playing the Carroll High School Dragons. Coach McGuire says that he knew that if they lost that game they were about to play, it really does feel like something. When you lose at the end of the season, that group of people, you're never going to play together again.
<v Ira Glass>在接下来的这个故事里，父母们显然已经站在了其中一边。这个故事通过两位妈妈之间的一系列信件展开。这是娜菲莎·汤普森-斯帕尔斯（Nafissa Thompson-Spires）一篇短篇小说的节选，首先由演员伊萨·戴维斯和埃丽卡·亚历山大朗读。

00:38:40.680 --> 00:38:54.100
<v McGuire>It's almost like there's a death. Most people don't talk about that today could be the last game. And so that was my point.
<v McGuire>这几乎就像是经历了死亡。大多数人不会去谈论今天可能是最后一场比赛。所以这就是我的重点。

00:38:54.100 --> 00:39:08.170
<v McGuire>This ain't a bunch of guys that play football! We just get to play football together because this is a brotherhood! This is a family! And man, whenever you have that, it's tough to destroy because you fight every single moment to keep it.
<v McGuire>这绝不仅仅是一群踢橄榄球的家伙！我们之所以能一起踢球，是因为这是一个兄弟会！这是一个大家庭！伙计们，只要你拥有这一切，它就很难被摧毁，因为你会为守住它而每时每刻去战斗。

00:39:08.170 --> 00:39:15.630
<v McGuire>That's what we got to do tonight. Because you win, we get another week together. And that's all I want. That's all I want. Is everybody with me?
<v McGuire>这就是我们今晚必须做到的。因为只要赢了，我们就能在一起多过一周。而这正是我想要的。这就是我想要的。大家跟我一起吗？

00:39:15.630 --> 00:39:19.270
<v Players>Yes, sir!
<v Players>是的，长官！

00:39:19.270 --> 00:39:23.330
<v Ira Glass>So the team went out there in front of all those people. And how'd it go?
<v Ira Glass>于是球队在所有人面前走上了赛场。结果怎么样？

00:39:23.330 --> 00:39:33.850
<v McGuire>We end up getting beat 37-35 right at the end of the game on an absolutely horrible call. But we ended up getting beat 37-35.
<v McGuire>我们最终在比赛快结束时以37比35输了，就因为一个极其糟糕的判罚。但我们最终还是以37比35输了。

00:39:33.850 --> 00:39:44.500
<v Ira Glass>Actually, it was a little worse than that-- 37-33. He says in the locker room afterward, it was really hard. But even if you win the state championship, which he has, that's hard, too.
<v Ira Glass>其实，比那还要惨一点——37比33。他说赛后在更衣室里，大家真的很难受。但即使你赢得了州冠军，他以前赢过，那也很难受。

00:39:44.500 --> 00:40:10.910
<v Ira Glass>Because that's the end. No more weeks together. Even if they win, there's that loss. He says he always makes it a point to thank the seniors, because it's often hardest for the seniors, you know, leaving the family.
<v Ira Glass>因为那是终点。再也没有在一起的几周了。即使他们赢了，也免不了那种失落感。他说他总是特意去感谢大四的学生，因为对大四学生来说，离开这个大家庭往往是最艰难的，你知道的。

00:40:10.910 --> 00:40:23.440
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, Sealed With a Diss. So sometimes the reason for a family meeting is a conflict that a kid is having at school. And then the parent has to decide, did my kid do something wrong or is the problem the other kid?
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，以不屑封缄。所以有时召开家庭会议的原因是孩子在学校遇到了冲突。然后家长必须决定，是我的孩子做错了什么，还是问题出在另一个孩子身上？

00:40:23.440 --> 00:40:38.380
<v Ira Glass>In this next story, the parents have definitely come down on one side of that one. The story unfolds in a series of letters between two moms. It's an excerpt of a short story by Nafissa Thompson-Spires, read first by actors Eisa Davis and Erika Alexander.
<v Ira Glass>在接下来这个故事里，家长们显然坚定地站在了其中一方。故事通过两位母亲之间的一系列信件展开。这是娜菲莎·汤普森-斯皮尔斯（Nafissa Thompson-Spires）短篇小说的一个片段，首先由演员埃萨·戴维斯（Eisa Davis）和埃rika·亚历山大（Erika Alexander）朗读。

00:40:38.380 --> 00:41:07.463
<v Ira Glass>(SUBJECT) EISA DAVIS AS LUCINDA: Tuesday, October 1, 1991. Hello, Monica. I'm sure you remember me from the class field trip to the Getty in September. It has been brought to my attention that your daughter Fatima may have started a nasty rumor about my Christinia. I hope to clear this up, as we both know how ugly these things can get.
<v Ira Glass>(主题) 伊萨·戴维斯饰露辛达：1991年10月1日，星期二。你好，莫妮卡。我确信你还记得我，我们九月份一起去盖蒂博物馆参加过班级远足。有人向我反映，你的女儿法蒂玛可能散布了关于我女儿克莉丝蒂娜的恶毒谣言。我希望能澄清这件事，因为我们都知道这种事情会变得多么难听。

00:54:41.380 --> 00:55:02.880
<v Ira Glass>Actors Erika Alexander and Eisa Davis reading an excerpt of "Belles Lettres," with Alvin Melathe reading Principal Lee. The story's by Nafissa Thompson-Spires. You can read the full story in her book of short stories, Heads of the Colored People.
<v Ira Glass>演员埃丽卡·亚历山大和伊萨·戴维斯朗读《美好书信》节选，阿尔文·梅拉特（Alvin Melathe）朗读李校长。这个故事的作者是娜菲莎·汤普森-斯帕尔斯。你可以在她的短篇小说集《有色人种的头脑》中阅读完整故事。

00:55:47.640 --> 00:56:06.590
<v Ira Glass>The program was produced today by Sean Cole and Bim Adewunmi. People who put together today's program include Michael Comite, Aviva DeKornfeld, Bethel Habte, Cassie Howley, Rudy Lee, Seth Lind, Katherine Rae Mondo, Nadia Reiman, Safiyah Riddle, Ryan Rumery, Alissa Shipp, Matt Tierney, and Diane Wu. Our managing editor is Sarah Abdurrahman. Our senior editor is David Kestenbaum. Our executive editor is Emanuele Berry.
<v Ira Glass>今天的节目由肖恩·科尔（Sean Cole）和布姆·阿德温米（Bim Adewunmi）制作。参与制作今天节目的人员包括迈克尔·科米特、阿维娃·德科恩费尔德、贝瑟尔·哈布特、卡西·豪利、鲁迪·李、塞斯·林德、凯瑟琳·雷·蒙多、纳迪亚·雷曼、萨菲亚·里德尔、瑞安·鲁梅里、阿莉莎·希普、马特·蒂尔尼和黛安·吴。我们的常务主编是萨拉·阿卜杜拉曼。我们的资深主编是大卫·克斯滕鲍姆。我们的执行主编是埃马纽埃莱·贝里。

00:56:06.590 --> 00:56:23.270
<v Ira Glass>Special thanks today to the entire LeBaron family, Eloy Bleifuss, Madeline Ticknor, PJ Mark, Heath Vanden Bosch, and kusports.com for video of Coach McGuire's speech. This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange.
<v Ira Glass>今天我们要特别感谢整个勒巴龙家族、埃洛伊·布莱富斯、马德琳·蒂克诺、PJ·马克、希思·范登·博施，以及提供麦圭尔教练演讲视频的kusports.com网站。《美国生活》由公共广播交换中心（PRX）分发给各公共广播电台。

00:56:23.270 --> 00:56:37.220
<v Ira Glass>Thanks as always to our program's confounder, Mr. Torey Malatia. You know, he was just cast in a local theater production of Annie? In the title role! So he actually adlibbed his way through a lot of the show, including Annie's biggest and most famous song, which he delivered as--
<v Ira Glass>一如既往地感谢我们节目的联合创始人托里·马拉蒂亚先生。你知道吗，他刚刚在当地的一部戏剧制作《安妮》中得到了一个角色！还是主角！所以他实际上在节目的很多部分都是即兴发挥，包括安妮最大、最著名的那首歌曲，他是这样唱的——

00:56:37.220 --> 00:56:40.200
<v Bryan>That's cool. What are we going to do tomorrow?
<v Bryan>太酷了。我们明天要做什么？

00:56:40.200 --> 00:56:45.200
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯。下周继续带给你更多《美国生活》的故事。
